- op: discuss  # a580
  at: '2026-09-06T19:38:24Z'  # a580
  by: agent  # a580
  actor: jgstern_agent  # a580
  clock: 4  # a580
  nonce: a580  # a580
  message: "OWNER RULING, 2026-09-06 (walk-through item 2 of 5). Owner: \"i confirm\" on the\nagent's decision memo, which was built from the ADRs, the spec, the tracker\nrecord and the commit history. RULED: (a) net_recv. Forward AND reverse\nlookups mint untrusted_input, per ADR-0049 ruling 1. No new boundary value\nnow; no host_info_read; no deferred-crossing shape.\n\nTHE RECORD THAT DECIDED IT (the premise of this item was wrong: the\nvocabulary was not silent):\n  1. erlang.yaml has rowed inet:getaddr/getaddrs/gethostbyname/gethostbyaddr\n     under net_recv since 2026-05-24 (WI-tukif, commit 0e2398a6f7), note\n     \"DNS resolution and service-name lookups (queries name servers)\". This\n     item's \"zero rows across go/rust/c/python\" grepped four catalogues, not\n     all. The choice was therefore parity-with-erlang vs retag-erlang, and\n     WI-pavob (c) rules that a vocabulary meaning different things per\n     language is the failure the axis discipline exists to prevent.\n  2. ADR-0049 ruling 1's own justification cites keeping `accept` because it\n     \"returns the peer address, network-controlled\": a network-chosen\n     ADDRESS is a transfer and mints. The format-constrained-address\n     argument was already subordinate when the ADR was written.\n  3. WI-vazal: a 28.2% source family (db_read -> db_write) was NOT split at\n     the label or the boundary because the split would silently flip 3 of\n     16 verdicts; the project carried source_boundary + flow_origins\n     instead. The record's answer to a noisy source family is report and\n     measure, not pre-split.\n  4. INV-tutar: env_read split AFTER measurement 0001 (22.9%, 134/195 rows\n     misfiled). ADR-0050's open question on `logging`: a new value goes\n     through an ADR-0024 family audit filed in docs/audits/, not an ad-hoc\n     ruling. Option (d) before a number is the explanation written before\n     the number.\n  5. (d) would also retag erlang's shipped minting rows -- a removal under\n     ADR-0049 ruling 3 (represented crossing + INV-buzab three-row check)\n     on top of the registry value; and the fs_read \"default quiet\"\n     precedent does not transfer (a file's sensitivity is what the program\n     stored; a resolver's answer is not the program's).\n\nWHAT SHIPS (one PR, parity test across languages):\n  - python: socket.gethostbyname / gethostbyname_ex / gethostbyaddr /\n    getaddrinfo / getnameinfo / getfqdn -> net_recv. Then the `socket`\n    module_completeness grant WI-dupok withheld on this item.\n  - go: net.LookupHost / LookupIP / LookupAddr / LookupCNAME / LookupMX /\n    LookupNS / LookupTXT / LookupSRV / LookupPort and net.Resolver's\n    Lookup* methods -> net_recv.\n  - c: netdb getaddrinfo / getnameinfo / gethostbyname / gethostbyaddr ->\n    net_recv.\n  - javascript: the `dns` module (lookup, resolve*, reverse; dns.promises)\n    -> net_recv. WI-nolut left dns unaudited, so parity is FIVE languages.\n  - rust: a `str` to_socket_addrs row for correctness only, INERT ON\n    ARRIVAL (WI-tuzaf: trait method on an untyped receiver); written, not\n    counted as coverage. WI-tuzaf stays open for the receiver problem.\n  - erlang: sort the existing row in the same pass. inet:gethostname is\n    the LOCAL hostname (c.yaml files gethostname under host_info_read);\n    getservbyname/getservbyport read the services table, not a name\n    server. Moving them out of net_recv is a REMOVAL under ruling 3:\n    represented crossing + INV-buzab three-row check, run not reasoned.\n  - repoint the INV-buzab examined-negative fixture that pins\n    socket.gethostbyname (test_verify_claims_examined_negative.py) at a\n    function that stays unrowed; keep gethostbyname as a positive control,\n    as ctypes and create_connection were.\n  - the PR ships the 0003 MARGINAL-PRECISION delta (baseline clone vs\n    subject, isolated caches, every added AND removed flow adjudicated\n    under ADR-0046's two-axis rubric) and carries awaits_bakeoff_validation.\n\nPRE-REGISTERED RE-OPEN TRIGGER, written before the number exists: if the\nadjudicated FORWARD-lookup flows are majority not-useful under ADR-0046\n(the lookup-then-connect idiom, getfqdn() / gethostbyname(gethostname())\nreaching logging), the forward-vs-reverse cut is filed as an ADR-0024\nfamily audit (docs/audits/<NN>-dns-lookup-family.md) and any new boundary\nis ruled on that number. Reverse lookups (a resolver-chosen NAME) stay\nnet_recv regardless.\n\nSequencing: off-arc; after Phase 6 / INV-busis unless the owner says\notherwise. Status -> todo_hard; needs_human_review tag removed."  # a580
